From a76cf74a673f3eacea0bd0d63161717788726984 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Audit <audit@local>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 02:22:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/7] Stop advertising three things the business does not offer
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

All three confirmed with Thomas. Each was a claim the site made that the
business could not honour, which costs more than the claim gains — a
customer who chooses on that basis finds out at the worst moment.

AFTERPAY. Listed among the accepted payment methods in two places (the
shared BookCta strip and the contact page's longer list) and not
accepted. Someone budgeting a yard revamp around paying in four would
have found out at the invoice. Removed from both; the remaining methods
are unchanged.

"CLEAR, PUBLISHED RATES." The Transparent Pricing card promised published
rates and then, in the same sentence, told the reader to contact us for a
quote. No rates are published anywhere on the site, and Thomas does not
want them published — jobs are quoted per property. The claim now
describes what actually happens: an honest quote up front, no surprises
on the invoice. Same reassurance, and true.

"ONLINE BOOKING." Four places said book online — the homepage trust bar,
its CTA button, the shared BookCta default label and lead, the footer
link, and the gallery CTA. Every one of them pointed at the contact form,
which takes an enquiry and starts a quote. Nothing on the services side
books anything. All now say quote.

Left alone deliberately: the contact page heading "Book a Job or Get a
Quote". You genuinely can request a job through that form, so it is
accurate as written.

Worth revisiting when the hire booking flow goes public — /hire IS a real
online booking, with live availability and a reservation. The claim
becomes true then, for that side of the business, and the copy can say so.

