From d8033690bc444ca9bcb3cb8cb70f536f465a39bf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Audit <audit@local>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 02:27:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] Closed Saturday everywhere; write down why the crons look
 wrong
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

HOURS. Four places state when the business is open and one of them
disagreed. The contact page advertised Saturday 7am-12pm and Sunday by
arrangement; CLOSED_DAY_INDICES in the hire config has both weekend days
shut; Doug tells customers the counter is closed Saturday and Sunday; and
the LocalBusiness schema says Mon-Fri. Settled as closed.

That was not merely untidy. The availability engine rejects any collection
or return on a closed day, so a DIY customer reading Saturday on the
contact page and then trying to book a wacker packer for Saturday morning
would have been refused by the form — the site advertising a service the
same site declines. All four now say the same thing.

The Google Business Profile has to match. A profile whose hours disagree
with the site it links to gets flagged, and the map pack is where a local
trade's calls come from, so this is worth checking rather than assuming.

CRONS. Both entries in vercel.json look like morning jobs and are not.
Vercel runs crons in UTC with no timezone option, so  is 08:30 UTC,
which is 18:00 in Adelaide. Six in the evening is the whole point of
job-reminders — it tells people about TOMORROW, so it wants to land after
the day's work rather than during it. Nothing in the repo said so, which
is why the audit initially read it as a ten-hour mistake. Now it does.

Both drift an hour from October to April when Adelaide moves to UTC+10:30
for daylight saving. Left alone and documented rather than engineered
around: an hour either side of six is still an evening reminder, and the
fixes cost more than the drift. A second cron entry is not available
(Hobby allows two, both used), and running hourly to check the local hour
would spend the remaining slot on a problem nobody has noticed.

Verified: tsc --noEmit passes.
