From b9617ef2a65955e6db707840f52aefc1630d269b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Audit <audit@local>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 02:32:55 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] Two ways a worker could be charged the same leave twice
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Both are the same shape: a check that reads state, then a write that does
not re-assert it. Neither has fired in production yet — daily_timesheets
has zero approved rows and leave_requests has one — so this is prevention,
not repair. Worth fixing before payroll runs in anger, because the symptom
is a worker being short on entitlement months later with nothing in the
data explaining why.

LEAVE APPROVAL. The route read the request, returned 409 if it was not
pending, then updated on id alone. Two admins actioning the same request,
or one admin double-tapping on a slow connection, both pass the read and
both reach increment_leave_balance. The update is now conditioned on
status = 'pending' so Postgres picks the winner, and .select() tells a
real claim from a loser since Supabase reports success either way.

TIMESHEET RE-APPROVAL. Worse, because it needs no race at all. The handler
selects approved_at and never looks at it, so approving a day a second
time — correcting the hours, fixing a note — ran the whole apply path
again. Paid hours survived that: worker_paid_hours upserts on
(worker_id, work_date), so a repeat is a no-op. The leave balance did not:
increment_leave_balance adds to a running total, so every re-approval
charged another day. The increment is now skipped when the day already
carried an approval.

Known gap, deliberately not fixed here: reclassifying an approved day from
one leave type to another (annual to sick, say) leaves the original
counter incremented, because unwinding it needs to know what the previous
approval was and the balance table records no such history. Skipping the
increment is strictly better than double-charging, but a reclassification
still wants a manual balance correction. Fixing it properly means either
an audit trail on the balances or recomputing them from timesheets, and
that is a design decision rather than a patch.

The hire reservation route already conditions its status transition the
same way (.eq("status", reservation.status)), which is where the pattern
came from.

Verified: tsc --noEmit passes.
