From 079f0e79afd48c6dfc939de2dd359ffcac5062f6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Audit <audit@local>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 02:38:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] System health: catch the silent failures instead of
 finding them months later
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

THE PROBLEM THIS SOLVES
Four failures ran silently for weeks. A migration written but never
applied — three weeks of review requests doing nothing. A OneSignal key
holding the App ID instead of the REST key — three months of push
notifications 401ing. A Resend domain never verified — three months of
enquiry emails 403ing. A cron that has never once run. Not one of them
threw anywhere a person looked, because the enquiry still SAVED every
time. The site looked fine. The thing that was broken was the part that
tells Thomas a lead came in.

That is the whole finding of both audits, and it is not a code-quality
problem. The code is careful. Nothing was watching it.

WHY NOT REUSE integrations.ts
Because it is what missed the outage. It answers "is X configured?" by
checking the env var is present, and ONESIGNAL_REST_API_KEY was present
the entire time — it just held the wrong value. Present is not correct.
Every probe here proves the thing WORKS: it calls Resend and reads back
whether the domain is verified, calls OneSignal with the REST key so a
wrong one comes back 401, calls Twilio, and reads a row that only exists
if the feature actually ran.

Eight checks: email, push, SMS, Xero, schema, crons, notification reach,
enquiry backlog. Each maps to a specific way this system has failed or
could fail without anyone noticing. Notification reach exists because a
push sent to zero subscribed devices SUCCEEDS — working and useless at
the same time. The Xero check warns at 40 days and fails at 50 because
Xero kills a refresh token at 60 days unused, so a quiet fortnight over
Christmas takes the invoicing down with no error anywhere.

ALERTS ON TRANSITION, NEVER ON STATE
A monitor that pings every day gets muted, and a muted monitor is worse
than none because it looks like coverage. This notifies when something
breaks and again when it recovers. Nothing in between.  tracks
when the current status began, so the message says "broken for 12 days"
rather than "broken" — the first prompts action, the second gets
postponed.

notified_status is written only AFTER attempting to send, so a failed
alert retries next run rather than being silently swallowed. A monitor
that loses its own alerts commits the exact fault it exists to catch.

Push and email fire independently: if push is the thing that broke, email
is the only way the message arrives, and vice versa.

NO NEW CRON SLOT
Vercel Hobby allows two cron entries and both are used. Health runs at
the end of job-reminders — last, and guarded, so the reminders people
depend on can never be delayed or broken by the monitor. A broken monitor
degrades to no monitor, never to a broken cron. Once a day is the right
cadence for "has anything quietly stopped", and piggybacking means the
monitor cannot itself be the thing that fails to get scheduled.

/admin/health reads stored state so the page opens instantly instead of
hammering four APIs on every refresh, with a "Run checks now" button for
the fix-and-verify loop — the loop that was missing when the OneSignal
key took three attempts to get right. That run passes notify:false: an
admin looking at the answer does not need to be pushed it.

VERIFIED AGAINST PRODUCTION
Ran the five database-backed probes against live data. Four return ok.
enquiry_backlog returns warn, correctly: 17 enquiries older than three
days with no job created, the oldest from 6 May. That is the real cost of
three months without notifications, and the new monitor found it on its
first run.

Verified: tsc --noEmit passes. Migration 0040 applied to production.
